  "image_name": "c65a58c4d6c24d90775d13bb806cf7e4.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure is a decision tree showing how to choose an electron microscopy approach based mainly on the sample’s physical state and pressure tolerance. It starts by asking whether the sample is gaseous, liquid/mixed, or solid. Gaseous samples are marked as generally not applicable, while solids go directly to standard solid-phase electron microscopy, where standard instrument settings can be used.\n\nFor liquid or mixed samples, the next key question is whether the liquid components are non-volatile. If they are, the figure points to electron microscopy in ionic liquids, again compatible with standard instrument configuration. If not, the chart asks whether the volatile components can survive at sub-atmospheric pressure. If they can, environmental electron microscopy is recommended, but this requires special equipment because the pressure must remain higher than in conventional vacuum operation.\n\nIf the volatile sample cannot tolerate reduced pressure, the final option considers whether observations are only possible under atmospheric conditions. In that case, the sample is placed in vacuum-tight cells. This route seems to allow use of a standard microscope, but it needs a special sample holder. Overall, the diagram compares methods by matching sample volatility and pressure requirements to the appropriate microscopy setup.",
